Spec: TenantRestoreScript

Platform: Rhai script library (vynil-core 0.7.7 engine)

Purpose:
  Orchestrate the immediate (no schedule branch exists here) restic restore of one tenant
    package inside a dispatched restore Pod: decode the Pod's own process env into the args map
    via @ctx::from_args, then walk a fixed @import_run stage sequence — pre-hook, the
    maintenance-start, the restic restore itself, the optional prepare hook, the five engine
    legs in secret → postgresql → mysql → mongodb → redis order, maintenance-stop, post-hook —
    where one package script can replace the whole lane and each engine leg can be individually
    replaced by its own package script. No CR is ever in hand: the instance is invisible at this
    level (frozen by /agent/src/tenant/restore.sdd), no status/condition/tag is written here, and
    the failure surface is exactly seven @throw strings over custom-script return codes plus
    whatever the stages re-throw. Diff-verified twin of ./../service/restore.rhai (the real diff
    is the single insert of the `restore_mongodb.sh` and `restore_redis.sh` gates with their
    module fall-throughs, between the mysql leg and maintenance_stop, every other byte
    identical): the only line of the twin's flow this file runs as SHIPPED modules — the tenant
    directory carries ./restore_mongodb.rhai and ./restore_redis.rhai that the service lane has
    no counterpart for — and thereby the only lane where the @ctx::from_args `mongo_list` /
    `redis_list` decodes reach a consumer. Stage-shape sibling of ./backup.rhai (spec pending).

Exposes:
  @run(@args) — the module's sole @fn and entry point; invoked as `restore::run(args)` by the
    Rust lane /agent/src/tenant/restore.sdd — the only module name its fixed snippet names is
    `restore` — whose `let _ =` discards the returned @Dynamic.

Accepts:
  @args — the ten-key CLI `args` map injected by the Rust lane. Read directly by this file:
    `package_dir` (all seven custom-script @is_file gates, spelled `${args.package_dir}`) plus,
    through the merged map, `s3_url` and `package_dir` (restore.sh command interpolation).
    @ctx::from_args mutates this SAME map in place and returns it as `context`, so the
    `args.package_dir` / `context.package_dir` spellings name one value — the 19-key env
    contract and `package_dir`'s untouched pass-through are owned by ./../lib/backup_context.sdd.
  @context downstream consumers (this file never reads the lists — it only says which stage
    runs): `snapshot` + `s3_url` + `restic_args` (consumed by ./restore_run.rhai — the
    WHAT-restores decision, env `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` copied verbatim), `secret_list` +
    `namespace` + the CLI-passed-through `template_dir` (./restore_secret.rhai), `pg_list`
    (./restore_postgresql.rhai), `mysql_list` (./restore_mysql.rhai), `mongo_list` plus its
    `${name}_dbname`/`_host`/`_username`/`_password` env family (./restore_mongodb.rhai, stage
    spec pending), `redis_list` + `namespace` plus the `${name}_sts`/`_password` envs
    (./restore_redis.rhai, stage spec pending), `deployment_list` / `statefulset_list` /
    `scale_target` (the maintenance pair's unwritten scale TO-DO only — read by no executed code
    today), while `restic_tags`, the four `max_*` retention keys, `sub_path` and `base_path`
    have no reader downstream in the tenant restore lane as wired (the latter two are consumed
    inside @ctx::from_args itself).
  Runtime inputs — seven optional package scripts under `${args.package_dir}/scripts/`:
    `restore.sh` (replaces the whole lane), `restore_prepare.sh` (an additive hook with NO
    shipped module and NO engine-bypass), `restore_secret.sh`, `restore_postgresql.sh`,
    `restore_mysql.sh`, `restore_mongodb.sh`, `restore_redis.sh` (per-leg replacements; the last
    two gates are tenant-only, twin-absent surface). Each gate is pure file existence; each
    executed script is a shell entry, not a Rhai module.

Returns:
  Custom-script path: @run hits `return rc` after `restore.sh` succeeds — `rc` there is
    necessarily 0 (a non-zero one threw first) — and the Rust lane discards it.
  Normal path: the implicit value is the trailing `import_run("restore_post", ...)` result
    (unit on a stock image, since `restore_post` ships nowhere); the Rust lane discards this
    too (@common/src/rhaihandler.sdd skip semantics).
  The observable result is entirely side-effect: a restic restore landing on `/` inside the
    Pod, Secrets re-applied through the cluster bridge, psql/mysql re-seeds from SQL dumps
    under `/backup`, a `mongorestore` archive load and a Redis `.rdb` pushed into the workload
    by the two tenant-only legs, and the 0-or-throw completion channel the Rust exit-5 lane
    reads.

Raises:
  Exactly seven @throw statements, all the same shape — template text
    `${context.package_dir}/scripts/<name> FAILED returning ${rc}` — one per custom script
    (`restore.sh`, `restore_prepare.sh`, `restore_secret.sh`, `restore_postgresql.sh`,
    `restore_mysql.sh`, `restore_mongodb.sh`, `restore_redis.sh`) on a non-zero @shell_run code.
    Throws from the restore.sh gate onward abort mid-lane; the thrown text reaches the process
    through the Rust lane's `Rhai script error:` prefix and exit-5 (frozen by
    /agent/src/tenant/restore.sdd — contrast backup's exit-4 — no condition, no
    `set_status_*` marker is minted at this level or by any shipped tenant stage).
  Stage re-throws propagate identically: @import_run swallows only the two skip markers
    (`No ${name} module, skipping.` / `No ${name}::run function, skipping.`; owners
    /common/src/rhaihandler.sdd + the vynil-core shim), so a stage throw aborts the run: a
    `restic backup FAILED returning ${rc}` from ./restore_run.rhai (the verbatim misnomer: the
    RESTORE stage throws "backup"), a `psql failed for ${pg}` from ./restore_postgresql.rhai,
    a `mysql restoration failed for ${mysql}` from ./restore_mysql.rhai, a cluster-bridge raise
    from ./restore_secret.rhai, a `mongorestore failed for ${mongo}` from
    ./restore_mongodb.rhai, or one of the six `... for ${redis}` strings (scale down /
    scale down wait / `No pod found for redis` / kubectl cp / rollout restart / rollout status)
    from ./restore_redis.rhai — the redis leg being the only executed code in this lane that
    touches workload replicas at all.
  Import-time: the module body's sole top-level statement `import "backup_context" as ctx;`
    resolves at module load; a resolution miss is a Rust-lane eval error before @run starts.
    @ctx::from_args itself raises for nothing env-side (./../lib/backup_context.sdd), and
    `context.s3_url` / `context.package_dir` are unguarded reads both always present (the
    former possibly the degraded `s3:/`, which the lane neither detects nor reports — it rides
    the restore.sh export and restic's own return codes downstream).

Handles:
  Total-lane custom restore — `restore.sh` present: run it with the repo export, then
    `import_run("restore_post")` and return — skipping the maintenance pair, restore_run, the
    prepare hook and all five engine legs. Absent (the stock case) falls through to the full
    sequence; `restore_pre` still ran before this gate either way.
  Snapshot choice — the env `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` is the sole WHAT-decider: @ctx::from_args copies
    it verbatim into `snapshot` and ./restore_run.rhai interpolates it straight into
    `restic restore ${context.snapshot} --exclude-xattr security.selinux --target /`; no
    latest-resolution, listing or filtering happens at this level, and an empty env still
    ships an empty snapshot token on the command line, leaving resolution and failure to
    restic's return code. The destination likewise arrives only as `BASE_REPO_URL` +
    `SUB_PATH` normalized into `s3_url`.
  Engine routing is file-existence ONLY, never list contents: each of the five legs takes its
    own script-or-module branch independently and in the fixed secret → postgresql → mysql →
    mongodb → redis order. The asymmetry against ./backup.rhai: the `restore_prepare.sh` gate
    has no else-branch — passing it adds its run but never bypasses the five legs (backup's
    twin gate DOES replace the block) — and no `restore_prepare` module ships to be shadowed.
    Conversely ./restore_run.rhai has no `.sh` replacement gate, and this lane has no
    backup_init / backup_check / backup_prune counterpart at all (nothing to init or verify:
    the repository already exists at restore time). Against ./../service/restore.rhai the two
    new gates are plain shape: the service twin simply has no mongodb/redis leg to replace.
  Maintenance window positions: @import_run("maintenance_start") sits right before
    restore_run and @import_run("maintenance_stop") after the redis leg — the window brackets
    the whole restore plus every engine re-seed, mongorestore and the redis rollout restart
    included. Both shipped modules are pass-through hook calls around a scale TO-DO comment —
    byte-identical twins frozen by ./../service/maintenance_windows.sdd (deployment_list/
    statefulset_list to 0 / back to `scale_target`): the window currently toggles nothing on
    the cluster, and this file itself never touches scale anywhere — `SCALE_TARGET` decodes
    into `scale_target` with no executed reader on either side of the TODO. The only real
    scale movement is the redis leg's own internal kubectl scale-down/restart/scale-back.
  Unshipped hook names: `restore_pre` and `restore_post` ship in no engine directory
    (./, ./../service, ./../lib) — on a stock image both take the debug-log skip; they exist
    as package `scripts/` slots. The two shipped tenant legs bracket further hook names of
    their own (`restore_mongodb_pre`/`_post`, `restore_redis_pre`/`_post`) that likewise ship
    nowhere — stage-side skip posture, owned by those pending stage specs.
  RESTIC environment handling — this file exports ONLY on the restore.sh line, the inline
    `export RESTIC_REPOSITORY="${context.s3_url}";` prefix; the other six custom scripts run
    bare `${context.package_dir}/scripts/<name>` with no export. TLS rides `restic_args`
    (`" --insecure-tls"`, appended by ./restore_run.rhai) and the Pod env; `RESTIC_HOST` and
    `RESTIC_TAGS` are never read or written here.
  No cron/schedule branch — scheduling lives in ./install.sdd and the ./schedule_backup.rhai
    lane (spec pending); this module, reached by `agent tenant restore` inside a dispatched
    restore Job or any process-env already shaped like one, always restores now.

Must:
  Execute exactly this order with nothing else interleaved: @ctx::from_args(args) →
    @import_run("restore_pre") → `restore.sh` gate (run + `restore_post` + `return` on presence,
    OR fall through) → @import_run("maintenance_start") → @import_run("restore_run") →
    `restore_prepare.sh` gate (run or skip; flow always continues) → `restore_secret.sh` gate
    (run, OR import the module) → `restore_postgresql.sh` gate (run, OR import) →
    `restore_mysql.sh` gate (run, OR import) → `restore_mongodb.sh` gate (run, OR import) →
    `restore_redis.sh` gate (run, OR import) → @import_run("maintenance_stop") →
    @import_run("restore_post") → end.
  Emit no log statement at this level (contrast ./backup.rhai's single
    `Starting backup using target:` line) — every log line of a stock restore comes from a
    stage module.
  Ship the restore.sh command with the exact prefix `export RESTIC_REPOSITORY="${context.s3_url}";`
    followed by `${context.package_dir}/scripts/restore.sh`, and ship the other six custom
    scripts as the bare `${context.package_dir}/scripts/<name>` command with no export.
  Treat any non-zero @shell_run code as fatal at its own throw site (never retried, never
    collected; `restore.sh` rc 0 alone returning).
  Pass the one `context` map to every @import_run and discard every return — the
    `context = import_run(...)` merge discipline of ./install.sdd is absent here, so stages
    communicate through their own side effects (restic, `/backup`, the cluster bridge, kubectl,
    shell env), never through a returned map.
  Resolve every stage name against the four resolver directories frozen by the Rust lane —
    `{package_dir}/scripts` first, then `{config_dir}`, `{script_dir}/tenant` (hard-coded kind
    segment), `{script_dir}/lib`, earlier entries winning an import — so a package or config
    `scripts/restore_run.rhai` (or any other stage name) replaces the shipped lane wholesale
    and the shipped `./restore_mongodb.rhai` / `./restore_redis.rhai` are the default legs no
    other engine directory could answer.
  Keep the gate presence check spelled `${args.package_dir}` while the command and throw text
    spell `${context.package_dir}` — two spellings, one value through the in-place merge.
  Keep the import-time surface at the single `import "backup_context" as ctx;` —
    ./../lib/backup_context.rhai executes nothing on load, so module load touches no env,
    filesystem or cluster.

Must not:
  Write any failure/success marker — no `set_status_*` call, no condition, no tag, no
    `instance` binding, no kube call: the Rust lane froze this side as CR-blind and any status
    write here would fabricate an object the restore Job never owns.
  Branch on env to pick scheduled-vs-immediate or to skip a stage wholesale: the only env
    influence on this lane is the DECODED env inside @from_args (destination, snapshot, engine
    lists, TLS), never a schedule or feature switch.
  Read `context.snapshot`, `mongo_list`, `redis_list` or any engine/scale list to route work —
    routing here is @is_file only and the lists are consumed by the stage modules, which self-
    no-op on empty ones.

Depends on:
  ./../lib/backup_context.rhai — the single import; @from_args half of the 19-key env→context
    contract (spec ./../lib/backup_context.sdd, which names this file among its from_args
    consumers).
  The shipped stage modules of this directory, all specs pending and each self-throwing:
    ./maintenance_start.rhai and ./maintenance_stop.rhai (no-op pair already frozen as twins
    by ./../service/maintenance_windows.sdd), ./restore_run.rhai (restic restore
    ${context.snapshot} --exclude-xattr security.selinux --target / ${context.restic_args}),
    ./restore_secret.rhai (Secret apply from `/backup/${secret}` files, target name from the
    `secret_${name}_target` env, hbs partials from `context.template_dir`),
    ./restore_postgresql.rhai (psql from `/backup/postgresql_${pg}[_cleaned].sql`),
    ./restore_mysql.rhai (mysql from `/backup/mysql_${mysql}[_cleaned].sql`),
    ./restore_mongodb.rhai (`mongorestore --archive=/backup/mongodb_${mongo}.archive` per
    `mongo_list` name, `--nsInclude` only for a non-`*` dbname), ./restore_redis.rhai
    (kubectl-driven per `redis_list` name: sts/deployment detection, scale-to-1, `kubectl cp`
    of `/backup/redis_${redis}.rdb`, rollout restart and wait, scale-back).
  vynil-core 0.7.7 engine API — the @import_run shim with its two skip markers, @is_file and
    @shell_run (int return code); registrars per /common/src/rhaihandler.sdd. This level
    calls no @log_info and no cluster function.

References:
  /agent/src/tenant/restore.sdd — the caller: the fixed `restore`-only snippet, ten-key
    `args`, the four-dir resolver order and the exit-5 lane every throw here lands in.
  ./../service/restore.rhai + ./../service/restore.sdd — the diff-verified twin: this flow
    without the mongodb/redis legs; its Tasks hold the open decisions on the identical
    surface, kept in lockstep here rather than re-decided.
  ./backup.rhai — the same-directory backup-lane sibling (spec pending): the
    block-replacing prepare gate, the export prefix on every custom script, the repo
    init/check/prune trio, and the mongodb/redis prepare legs this restore lane answers.
  /agent/tests/rhai_backup_redis.rs — the nearest test file (Tasks; mock engine points at
    scripts/lib + scripts/tenant — the tenant resolver pair — but drives stage modules only).

Scenario: custom restore.sh replaces the whole lane
  Given `${args.package_dir}/scripts/restore.sh` exists in the mounted package scripts
  When @run executes and the script exits 0
  Then the RESTIC_REPOSITORY-exported copy ran, @import_run("restore_post") followed and @run
    returned the rc — maintenance_start, restore_run, the prepare hook, all five engine legs
    and maintenance_stop never ran
  But given the script exits 3
  Then the throw text is `${context.package_dir}/scripts/restore.sh FAILED returning 3` and no
    restore_post import runs

Scenario: snapshot env decides the restored snapshot verbatim
  Given process env `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` set to `abcd1234` with the repo envs present
  When the stock flow reaches ./restore_run.rhai
  Then `restic restore abcd1234 --exclude-xattr security.selinux --target /` runs with the
    merged `restic_args` — this file never inspects, validates or latest-resolves the token
  But given `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` is unset
  Then `snapshot` is `""`, the empty token still rides the command line, and restic's own
    non-zero rc surfaces as the misnamed stage throw — no fail-fast at either level

Scenario: five-leg engine ladder order and per-leg replacement
  Given no `restore_prepare.sh` and no per-leg scripts
  When @run passes the restore_run step
  Then @import_run fires secret → postgresql → mysql → mongodb → redis in that fixed order on
    the one context map, whatever the five lists hold (empty lists still import_run, each
    stage's loop no-ops; a stock image SHIPS the last two legs, which the service twin lacks)
  Given the same package ships `restore_redis.sh` only
  Then the redis leg is the bare shell run and the other four legs stay module imports

Scenario: restore_prepare.sh is additive, not a block replacement
  Given `restore_prepare.sh` exists and exits 0
  Then the five engine legs still run afterwards — the gate has no else-branch and no
    `restore_prepare` module exists for it to shadow
  But given it exits 5
  Then the throw names `restore_prepare.sh FAILED returning 5` before any engine leg runs

Scenario: stock image skips unshipped hooks and returns via the post-hook
  Given a package with no custom scripts, empty engine lists and the stock engine directory set
  When @run completes the normal path
  Then `restore_pre` and `restore_post` hit the two debug-log skip markers (they ship nowhere),
    the five stage modules import, run their own `_pre`/`_post` skips and no-op loops, and
    @run returns that unit
  And this module emitted no log of its own — every `Starting ... restore` line in the output
    belongs to the stage modules (./restore_mongodb.rhai and ./restore_redis.rhai log per
    non-empty list name)

Scenario: stage throw aborts mid-window
  Given the sequence reached ./restore_redis.rhai with one list entry, and its
    `kubectl rollout restart` exits 1
  Then the stage's own `rollout restart failed for ${redis}` propagates through @import_run —
    the stage's scale-back, maintenance_stop and the restore_post hook never run and the Rust
    lane exits 5 behind `Rhai script error: `

Tasks:
  [ ] Map the six Scenarios to tests: /agent/tests/rhai_backup_redis.rs runs a mock engine
      over scripts/lib + scripts/tenant but names only stage modules — nine `restore_redis`
      and five `backup_prepare_redis` tests calling `rs::run` directly — so the tenant
      restore STAGE gets coverage while this orchestrator gets none (no test imports
      `restore`), the `restore_mongodb` stage gets none either, and the stage specs the
      existing tests pin are still pending. A mock over the full four-dir resolver list
      (package scripts, config, tenant, lib) is what this orchestrator's Scenarios need.
  [?] Twin-mirrored open questions — the restore_run misnomer (the
      `restic backup FAILED returning ${rc}` text on a RESTORE), `restore_prepare.sh`
      additive-vs-block, the export asymmetry (only
      the restore.sh command carries `RESTIC_REPOSITORY`; the other six custom scripts run
      bare), restore_pre/restore_post shipping nowhere, the restore.sh replacement skipping
      maintenance_stop with the rc-0 return discarded, and an empty `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` building a
      live `restic restore ... --target /` — are all unresolved in ./../service/restore.sdd
      Tasks over the diff-verified identical surface; decide them there in lockstep and copy
      the verdicts here (each one now also covers the mongodb/redis leg custom scripts).
  [?] The maintenance window toggles nothing while the redis leg scales its own workload up
      and back down by itself via shell kubectl (stage spec pending): the bracket is decoration
      for the other four engines, whose full-filesystem restore and DB re-seeds run against
      live workloads. Decide whether the scale TO-DO frozen in
      ./../service/maintenance_windows.sdd absorbs the redis self-scale pattern generically
      (then the leg's internal scaling
      retires) or per-leg self-scaling is the intended architecture (then the window retires).

Done when:
  Every Scenario carries a passing mock-engine test against ./restore.rhai (resolver list
    including scripts/tenant), the frozen order and the seven throw texts still match the
    source, and `specdd lint` is green on this file.
